{
  "format": "Shared by the TypeScript (api/__tests__/futures-contracts.test.ts) roll-forward rule tests and by the Python (uw-stream, Task 6d) tests, which are to run these same cases. To run a case: build one list of rows per contract key, in order of first appearance in the segments. A segment is k consecutive minutes starting at `from`; in each of them every contract named in `volumes` gets one row (ts, volume), and a contract not named has no row that minute. All timestamps are UTC minutes in the form YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:00.000Z. A volume of 0 is still a row. Minutes between segments have no rows at all. Run the rule with the case's `root` and `seed` (null means no seed). Keys are contract symbols as fed: the rule folds a key to its one-digit name (ESZ26 is ESZ6), so `takeovers` and `front` use the one-digit names. The seed is folded the same way: with rows, a two-digit seed outside the window below makes the rule throw (that is a caller bug and has no field here); with no row at all it is folded by its last digit. Compare the result to `takeovers` (ts is the first minute on the new contract, in time order), `front` (the front after the last row, for the minute that follows it; null only when `seed` is null and no row of a key the rule keeps exists, so there is nothing to date a cold-start seed; a cold-start seed is dated at the first minute of a kept key, not at a skipped key's earlier row) and `skipped` (keys the rule leaves out, sorted ascending by code point: not an outright of the root, or a two-digit year outside the one-digit window [R - 1, R + 8], R being the UTC year of the first row of any outright key; such a year has no one-digit name that would not collide with a nearer contract) exactly. When a case has `emitted`, also compare it: every row the rule emits, in time order, each `{ts, contract, key, volume}`, where `contract` is the canonical name, `key` is the key (spelling) whose own row it is and `volume` is that row's own volume, not the sum of the minute's spellings. To check `key`, tag each row you build with the key it came from. `N` is the guard length in minute closes.",
